"""English — details, beginner_4 (50 questions)."""

D = {
"quran_151": "Open any mushaf at its index and the count is there: one hundred and fourteen surahs, beginning with Al-Fatiha and ending with An-Nas. The Qur'an itself uses the word surah for its chapters, calling Surah An-Nur \"a surah which We have sent down and made obligatory\" (24:1), and it challenges doubters to \"produce a surah like it\" (2:23). Some surahs run to hundreds of verses and some to three. A Muslim who learns the number 114 learns the shape of the Book he will read all his life.",
"quran_152": "Al-Fatiha stands at the head of every copy of the Qur'an, and every Muslim who prays recites it in each rak'ah, so it is the surah a child learns first. It gathers praise, \"All praise is due to Allah, Lord of the worlds\" (1:2), a promise, \"You alone we worship and You alone we ask for help\" (1:5), and a request, \"Guide us to the straight path\" (1:6). Its name means the opening, because the Book opens with it. A Muslim opens his prayer with the same seven verses.",
"quran_153": "The Qur'an names itself with the very word for reciting. In Surah Al-Qiyama Allah says of the revelation: \"Indeed, upon Us is its collection and its recitation (qur'anahu). So when We have recited it, then follow its recitation\" (75:17-18). The first words sent down to the Prophet ﷺ were \"Read, in the name of your Lord who created\" (96:1), and the dawn prayer is called \"the recitation of dawn\" (17:78). A Muslim learns from the name itself that the Qur'an is a Book meant to be read aloud.",
"quran_154": "The first verses came to the Prophet ﷺ in the cave of Hira when he was forty, and the last came shortly before he died at sixty-three; every standard biography counts those twenty-three years. The Qur'an explains why it did not come all at once: \"a Qur'an which We have separated, that you may recite it to the people over a prolonged period\" (17:106), and \"that We may strengthen your heart thereby\" (25:32). A Muslim learns that the Book came verse by verse, as the community needed it.",
"quran_155": "The word aya means a sign, and the Qur'an uses it for two things at once: the signs Allah places in creation, \"And of His signs is the creation of the heavens and the earth\" (30:22), and the sentences of the Book, \"a Book whose verses (ayat) are perfected\" (11:1). Each aya carries a number in the mushaf, so a verse can be found as 2:255 or 112:1. A Muslim learns that every verse he reads is called a sign because it points him to Allah.",
"quran_156": "The Qur'an uses the word surah for its own chapters. It says of Surah An-Nur: \"A surah which We have sent down and made obligatory\" (24:1), and to those who doubted the Book: \"Then bring ten surahs like it\" (11:13). Each surah takes its name from something inside it: Al-Baqara from the cow Musa's people were told to sacrifice (2:67), Al-Kahf from the cave of the young men (18:9). A Muslim finds his way around the Qur'an by these one hundred and fourteen names.",
"quran_157": "Al-Baqara, the second surah, has 286 verses, more than any other. Its name comes from the cow that Musa's people were commanded to sacrifice (2:67). It holds the Verse of the Throne (2:255) and the longest single verse of the Qur'an, the verse on writing down a debt (2:282). It closes with the prayer: \"Our Lord, do not burden us with more than we can bear\" (2:286). A Muslim who reads it meets law, stories and prayer in one surah.",
"quran_158": "Al-Kawthar is surah 108, and the whole of it can be recited in one breath: \"Indeed, We have granted you al-Kawthar. So pray to your Lord and sacrifice. Indeed, your enemy is the one cut off\" (108:1-3). It is addressed to the Prophet ﷺ, and it turns a gift into a duty: because Allah has given, the Prophet ﷺ is to pray and sacrifice for Him alone. A Muslim learns from its three verses that the answer to a gift from Allah is worship.",
"quran_159": "The basmala, \"In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate\", stands at the head of every surah except At-Tawba, so a reader meets it 113 times. It also appears inside a surah, opening the letter Sulayman sent to the queen of Saba': \"Indeed, it is from Sulayman, and indeed, it is: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate\" (27:30). The first revelation began with Allah's name too (96:1). A Muslim says the basmala before reading, eating and beginning any task.",
"quran_160": "The verse belongs to the opening of Surah At-Tawba, where Allah sets out how the believers are to deal with the polytheists of Makkah: \"And if any one of the polytheists seeks your protection, then grant him protection so that he may hear the words of Allah; then deliver him to his place of safety\" (9:6). Even an enemy is kept safe so that he can listen, because what he hears is the speech of Allah Himself. A Muslim handles the Qur'an with care because he holds Allah's own words.",
"quran_161": "Every printed mushaf marks the thirty ajza' in its margins, so a reader can measure his progress. The division follows the Qur'an's own instruction: \"So recite what is easy of the Qur'an\" (73:20). Ramadan is \"the month in which the Qur'an was sent down\" (2:185), and one juz' a night finishes the Book by its end. A Muslim keeps the whole Qur'an in his life a portion at a time.",
"quran_162": "Surah 19 tells Maryam's story from inside her own life: \"And mention in the Book Maryam, when she withdrew from her family to a place toward the east\" (19:16), the angel's news of a pure boy, and her return carrying 'Isa, who spoke from the cradle (19:29-30). Elsewhere the angels say to her: \"O Maryam, indeed Allah has chosen you and purified you and chosen you above the women of the worlds\" (3:42). A Muslim learns her name as a surah he can read.",
"quran_163": "The two surahs are twins in their opening words. Al-Falaq begins: \"Say, I seek refuge in the Lord of daybreak\" (113:1), and An-Nas begins: \"Say, I seek refuge in the Lord of mankind\" (114:1). The Arabic verb for seeking refuge, a'udhu, gives them their shared name, al-Mu'awwidhatan. Al-Falaq asks protection from darkness, from those who blow on knots and from the envier; An-Nas asks protection from the whisperer who retreats. A Muslim recites the two of them for safety, and they close the whole Qur'an.",
"quran_164": "After the basmala, the first verse of Al-Fatiha reads: \"All praise is due to Allah, Lord of the worlds\" (1:2). Al-hamdu means praise and thanks together, and the Qur'an opens with it before any request is made. Four other surahs begin with the same words, Al-An'am (6:1), Al-Kahf (18:1), Saba' (34:1) and Fatir (35:1), and the people of Paradise end their prayer with them: \"praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds\" (10:10). A Muslim learns to put praise before asking, exactly in the order of the Fatiha.",
"quran_165": "Tafsir is the work of explaining what a verse means: its words, the story behind it, and how its parts fit together. The Qur'an itself uses the word when it says that Allah answers every objection with \"the truth and the best explanation (tafsir)\" (25:33). The Prophet ﷺ was the first to explain the Book, as Allah told him: \"that you may make clear to the people what was sent down to them\" (16:44). A Muslim reads tafsir so that the verses he recites become verses he understands.",
"quran_166": "Surah At-Tawba, the ninth surah, begins straight away with its first verse, without the basmala: \"A declaration of disassociation from Allah and His Messenger to those with whom you had made a treaty among the polytheists\" (9:1). A reader who finishes Al-Anfal moves directly into At-Tawba with no line of separation. The surah is also known as Bara'a, from that first word; its name At-Tawba comes from the verses in which Allah turns in mercy to the believers (9:117-118). A Muslim recognises the surah by its missing basmala.",
"quran_167": "The word qari' comes from the same root as Qur'an, qara'a, to read or recite, so the reciter's name and the Book's name are cousins. The first command given to the Prophet ﷺ was exactly this: \"Read\" (96:1). The Qur'an also tells how the reading should be done: \"And recite the Qur'an with measured recitation\" (73:4), and how it should be received: \"So when the Qur'an is recited, then listen to it and pay attention\" (7:204). A Muslim who learns to recite well becomes a qari' in his own home.",
"quran_168": "The verse reads: \"Allah, there is no deity except Him, the Ever-Living, the Sustainer of all existence. Neither drowsiness overtakes Him nor sleep. To Him belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth. His Throne extends over the heavens and the earth, and their preservation tires Him not. And He is the Most High, the Most Great\" (2:255). A Muslim learns Ayat al-Kursi by heart as one verse that describes Allah completely.",
"quran_169": "Surah 24 takes its name from a verse in its middle: \"Allah is the Light of the heavens and the earth. The example of His light is like a niche within which is a lamp, the lamp within a glass, the glass as if it were a brilliant star\" (24:35). The same verse ends: \"Allah guides to His light whom He wills\" (24:35), and the next describes houses in which His name is remembered morning and evening (24:36). A Muslim thinks of guidance as a light that Allah gives.",
"quran_170": "Surah 55 opens with the name itself and lists what the Most Merciful has done: \"The Most Merciful. Taught the Qur'an. Created man. Taught him speech\" (55:1-4). It then goes through the sun and moon, the sea, the fruits and Paradise, and after each gift asks the same question thirty-one times: \"So which of the favours of your Lord would you deny?\" (55:13). Ar-Rahman is one of the two names of mercy in the basmala. A Muslim learns to look at the world as a list of favours.",
"quran_171": "Surah 18 is named after the cave in which a group of young believers took shelter from a king who opposed their faith: \"When the youths retreated to the cave and said, 'Our Lord, grant us from Yourself mercy'\" (18:10). Allah made them sleep, and the Qur'an gives their stay as three hundred years and nine (18:25). The same surah tells of Musa's journey with a servant of Allah given knowledge (18:65), and of Dhul-Qarnayn (18:83). A Muslim reads Al-Kahf and finds stories of faith kept safe through hardship.",
"quran_172": "Surah 4 is named An-Nisa', the women, because so many of its verses deal with the rights of women, orphans and families. It opens with the origin of every human being: \"O mankind, fear your Lord, who created you from one soul and created from it its mate, and dispersed from both of them many men and women\" (4:1). It gives women a fixed share of inheritance (4:7). A Muslim learns that a whole surah stands guard over the rights of women.",
"quran_173": "The name comes from the last story in surah 5, when the disciples of 'Isa asked: \"O 'Isa, son of Maryam, can your Lord send down to us a table from the heaven?\" (5:112). 'Isa prayed: \"O Allah, our Lord, send down to us a table from the heaven to be for us a festival and a sign from You\" (5:114), and Allah answered that He would send it. A Muslim learns that a table of food can be a sign from Allah.",
"quran_174": "Surah 71 is given wholly to Nuh and his people. It begins: \"Indeed, We sent Nuh to his people, saying, 'Warn your people before there comes to them a painful punishment'\" (71:1), and lets Nuh tell of his long effort: \"My Lord, indeed I invited my people night and day, but my invitation increased them only in flight\" (71:5-6). The surah ends with his prayer for his parents and every believer who entered his house (71:28). A Muslim learns from Surah Nuh what patience in calling to Allah looks like.",
"quran_175": "The seven verses of Al-Fatiha are short enough to count on one hand, and every Muslim recites them in each rak'ah of every prayer. They move from praise of Allah, \"Lord of the worlds\" (1:2), \"the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate\" (1:3), \"Master of the Day of Judgement\" (1:4), to a promise, \"You alone we worship and You alone we ask for help\" (1:5), to a request, \"Guide us to the straight path\" (1:6). A Muslim who prays five times a day says these seven verses at least seventeen times.",
"quran_176": "The words close Surah Al-Buruj: \"But this is a glorious Qur'an\" (85:21), \"in a Preserved Tablet\" (85:22). The surah has just told of the people of the ditch, believers burned for their faith, and warned those who deny the truth (85:19-20); then it declares that the Book they reject is kept safe with Allah. Another surah says: \"Indeed, it is We who sent down the message, and indeed, We will be its guardian\" (15:9). A Muslim learns that the Qur'an in his hands is protected by its Sender.",
"quran_177": "Surah 54 opens with the moon itself: \"The Hour has come near, and the moon has split\" (54:1). It then recalls the peoples who denied their messengers, and after each story repeats the same invitation: \"And We have certainly made the Qur'an easy for remembrance, so is there any who will remember?\" (54:17), four times in all. A Muslim learns from Al-Qamar that the Qur'an is made easy to learn, and that the moon was a sign given to make people remember.",
"quran_178": "Surah 75 opens with an oath on the day it is named for: \"I swear by the Day of Resurrection\" (75:1). It answers those who doubt that a scattered body can be brought back: \"Does man think that We will not assemble his bones? Yes, We are able even to proportion his fingertips\" (75:3-4). In its middle it tells the Prophet ﷺ not to hurry his tongue with the revelation, because its collection is upon Allah (75:16-17). A Muslim learns that Allah, who made the fingertips, can remake the whole.",
"quran_179": "Surah 14 carries Ibrahim's name because it holds his long prayer for Makkah and his family. He asks: \"My Lord, make this city secure and keep me and my sons away from worshipping idols\" (14:35), and explains: \"Our Lord, I have settled some of my descendants in an uncultivated valley near Your sacred House\" (14:37). He thanks Allah for Isma'il and Ishaq given in old age (14:39) and asks that his descendants keep up the prayer (14:40). A Muslim learns Ibrahim's prayer as the beginning of the story of Makkah.",
"quran_180": "Surah 57 takes its name from a verse that counts iron among Allah's gifts: \"And We sent down iron, in which is great strength and benefits for the people\" (57:25). The same verse says that Allah sent the messengers with clear proofs, the Scripture and the balance, so that people would stand up for justice. The surah opens: \"Whatever is in the heavens and the earth exalts Allah\" (57:1). A Muslim learns that even the iron in a nail is among the favours of Allah.",
"virtues_101": "The verse comes in Surah Al-Isra, joined to the command to worship Allah alone: \"Whether one or both of them reach old age with you, say not to them a word of annoyance and do not repel them, but speak to them a noble word\" (17:23). The next verse adds a prayer: \"My Lord, have mercy upon them as they brought me up when I was small\" (17:24). A Muslim learns that even the smallest sound of impatience is too much for a parent.",
"virtues_102": "The Qur'an uses the word sadaqa for what a person gives from his own wish. It says: \"If you disclose your charities, it is good; but if you conceal them and give them to the poor, it is better for you\" (2:271), and pictures such a gift as a seed that grows seven ears (2:261). Unlike zakat, sadaqa has no set amount and no set time. A Muslim learns that a coin, a meal or a shared toy given for Allah's sake all count as sadaqa.",
"virtues_103": "The hadith recorded in Sahih al-Bukhari 6018 joins three duties to one condition: the Prophet ﷺ said that whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day should not harm his neighbour, should honour his guest, and should say what is good or keep silent. The Qur'an gives the same weight to words: \"Not a word does he utter except that with him is an observer prepared\" (50:18). A Muslim learns to test a sentence before saying it: if it is not good, silence is better.",
"virtues_104": "The hadith recorded in Sahih al-Bukhari 6015 belongs to the chapter of good manners in which the Prophet ﷺ tied kindness to the neighbour to belief in Allah and the Last Day, beside honouring the guest and speaking good or keeping silent. The Qur'an had already set the neighbour among those owed kindness: \"to parents do good, and to relatives, orphans, the needy, the near neighbour, the neighbour farther away\" (4:36). A Muslim learns that the family next door has a claim on him that his faith demands.",
"virtues_105": "The verse gives the believers two helpers together: \"O you who have believed, seek help through patience and prayer. Indeed, Allah is with the patient\" (2:153). Two verses later Allah says what the patience is for: \"We will surely test you with something of fear and hunger, but give good tidings to the patient, who, when disaster strikes them, say, 'Indeed we belong to Allah, and indeed to Him we will return'\" (2:155-156). A Muslim learns that being patient is not being alone.",
"virtues_106": "Surah Ad-Duha is addressed to the Prophet ﷺ himself, and just before this command Allah reminds him of his own childhood: \"Did He not find you an orphan and give you refuge?\" (93:6). Then come three instructions that answer three gifts: \"So as for the orphan, do not oppress him. And as for the one who asks, do not repel him. But as for the favour of your Lord, speak of it\" (93:9-11). A Muslim learns that whoever received kindness when small owes it to the orphan in his turn.",
"virtues_107": "The command comes among the verses of Surah Al-Hajj that describe the pilgrimage: \"So avoid the uncleanliness of idols and avoid false speech\" (22:30). A lie is placed in the same sentence as an idol, as something a believer keeps well away from. The Qur'an praises the servants of the Most Merciful as those \"who do not witness falsehood\" (25:72), and commands: \"be with those who are true\" (9:119). A Muslim learns that telling the truth is part of keeping his worship clean.",
"virtues_108": "The verse follows straight after the verses on parents in Surah Al-Isra: \"And give the relative his right, and the poor and the traveller, and do not spend wastefully\" (17:26). The next verse explains why waste is named alongside giving: \"Indeed, the wasteful are brothers of the devils\" (17:27). Wealth is to travel outward, to family first, then to the poor and the stranger far from home. A Muslim learns that what he gives is called a right, something owed, not a favour.",
"virtues_109": "The promise is recalled by Musa to his people in Surah Ibrahim. He reminds them how Allah saved them from Pharaoh's people (14:6), then repeats what their Lord had declared: \"If you are grateful, I will surely increase you; but if you deny, indeed, My punishment is severe\" (14:7). The word for giving thanks, shukr, is the same word by which the Qur'an calls the family of Dawud to \"work in gratitude\" (34:13). A Muslim says al-hamdu lillah for what he has because the Qur'an ties thankfulness to receiving more.",
"virtues_110": "The Qur'an names sabr again and again as the believer's strength. \"O you who have believed, seek help through patience and prayer\" (2:153); \"Indeed, the patient will be given their reward without account\" (39:10); and to the Prophet ﷺ: \"And be patient, and your patience is not but through Allah\" (16:127). Luqman gives it to his son as advice: \"be patient over what befalls you\" (31:17). A Muslim learns as-sabr as a word to say to himself when something is hard.",
"faith_191": "The Qur'an asks for dhikr in many verses and promises what comes of it. \"So remember Me; I will remember you\" (2:152); \"O you who have believed, remember Allah with much remembrance\" (33:41); and: \"Verily, in the remembrance of Allah do hearts find rest\" (13:28). In daily practice, dhikr is the subhan Allah, al-hamdu lillah and Allahu akbar said after every prayer; it needs no place, no time and no wealth.",
"faith_192": "The Islamic calendar counts its years from the Prophet's ﷺ migration from Makkah to Madinah, which is why it is called hijri, and each year begins with Muharram, as any hijri calendar shows. The Qur'an fixes the number of months: \"Indeed, the number of months with Allah is twelve months in the register of Allah; of these, four are sacred\" (9:36). Muharram is one of those four, and its name itself means sacred. A Muslim learns that his year opens with a month set apart for peace.",
"faith_193": "The Qur'an calls for tawba and promises that it is accepted. \"O you who have believed, repent to Allah with sincere repentance\" (66:8); \"Indeed, Allah loves those who are constantly repentant\" (2:222); and to those who fear it is too late: \"do not despair of the mercy of Allah. Indeed, Allah forgives all sins\" (39:53). The first tawba in the Qur'an is Adam's: \"Then Adam received from his Lord some words, and He accepted his repentance\" (2:37). A Muslim learns that no sin closes the door back to Allah.",
"faith_194": "The verse opens a passage of Surah Hud on how completely Allah knows His creation: \"And there is no creature on earth but that upon Allah is its provision, and He knows its place of dwelling and place of storage. All is in a clear register\" (11:6). The word dabba covers every animal that moves on the ground, the ant as much as the camel; Allah provides for each one (29:60). A Muslim works for his living while trusting that provision comes from Allah.",
"faith_195": "The Qur'an names the city and its House: \"Indeed, the first House established for mankind was that at Bakkah, blessed and a guidance for the worlds,\" and in the next verse: \"due to Allah from the people is a pilgrimage to the House for whoever is able to find a way\" (3:96-97). Ibrahim and Isma'il raised its foundations (2:127), and the Qur'an also speaks of \"the valley of Makkah\" (48:24). A Muslim turns towards Makkah five times a day and hopes to travel there once.",
"faith_196": "The Arabic word hajj names the pilgrimage, and the one who performs it is called a hajj, or hajji, from the same root. The Qur'an tells Ibrahim: \"And proclaim to the people the hajj; they will come to you on foot and on every lean camel, coming from every distant pass\" (22:27), and fixes its season: \"The hajj is during well-known months\" (2:197). After returning home, a pilgrim is often addressed by that title. A Muslim learns the word as a name of honour earned by answering Ibrahim's call.",
"faith_197": "The verse comes early in Surah Al 'Imran: \"Say, whether you conceal what is in your breasts or reveal it, Allah knows it. And He knows that which is in the heavens and that which is on the earth\" (3:29). Another surah says Allah is closer to a person than his jugular vein (50:16). A Muslim learns that a thought kept inside is already known, so the heart deserves as much care as the tongue.",
"faith_198": "The verse is the first of a group in Surah Al-Baqara that lays down the fast: \"O you who have believed, fasting is prescribed for you as it was prescribed for those before you, that you may become righteous\" (2:183). The verses that follow name the month, \"the month of Ramadan in which was revealed the Qur'an\" (2:185), and the hours, from the white thread of dawn until the night (2:187). A Muslim learns that when he fasts he joins a line of believers reaching back long before him.",
"faith_199": "Surah Al-Jumu'a commands the Friday prayer: \"O you who have believed, when the call is made for the prayer on the day of Jumu'a, hasten to the remembrance of Allah and leave trade\" (62:9). The next verse releases the people afterwards: \"And when the prayer has been concluded, disperse within the land and seek from the bounty of Allah\" (62:10). In every mosque a sermon comes first, then two rak'ahs in place of Dhuhr. A Muslim learns Friday as the day the neighbourhood prays together.",
"faith_200": "The Qur'an commands the fast with this word: \"O you who have believed, fasting (as-siyam) is prescribed for you\" (2:183), and fixes its month: \"whoever sights the month, let him fast it\" (2:185). The root of the word means to hold back, and the Qur'an uses it in that sense for Maryam too: \"I have vowed to the Most Merciful a fast (sawm), so I will not speak today to any person\" (19:26). A Muslim learns that as-sawm means holding back from food and drink for the sake of Allah.",
}
